      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Merrin & Sallow Architects, a fictional practice. You're Tomás Carvalho, PA to the managing partner, Catherine Merrin, and she's travelling today. Five emails and voicemails are waiting. Some are real and some were made to trick you, including one that sounds just like her. Sort them, and check or report anything that doesn't sit right. Reporting or checking when you're unsure is always fine and never marked down, though a real one does take a colleague's time to check.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It read as an ordinary message from Catherine, with nothing in it to question. Next, messages like it arrive in Tomás's inbox and voicemail, and you're the one reading them.",
        "noBlameLine": "Every one of these was built to look and sound right, and the people who act on them are trying to help in a hurry. A miss here is about the pressure you were under, and it shows where to look next time.",
        "defendIntro": "Now you're in Tomás's seat. Five emails and voicemails are waiting. Take them one at a time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if someone senior asks you to pay, buy or send something in a hurry, or to keep it from finance, check before you act, even when it's their voice on a voicemail. Ring them on the number already in your contacts, or ask finance, then report the message using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These messages were made by people whose job is to make them look and sound right, and anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "voicePanel": {
          "heading": "A voice is not proof",
          "text": "A voice can be copied. Recordings of someone speaking in public, such as a talk or an interview, can be used to make new sentences in a voice that sounds like theirs. So a familiar voice on a voicemail or a call doesn't tell you who is speaking. What still works is the route: ring the person back on a number you already hold, or check with someone who would know, such as finance."
        }
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
    }
